    "en":  {
               "headline":  "IREN hits 52-week low as Microsoft delay, $6B funding gap meet AI selloff",
               "technical":  "The trend is clearly down. Price closed at $11.82, far below all three exponential moving averages (EMAs, which track the average price and give more weight to recent days): EMA50 at $27.78, EMA100 at $34.01 and EMA200 at $37.68. The averages are stacked in bearish order, with the shortest on the bottom, which is a classic downtrend. MACD (a trend-momentum gauge) is -4.64, below its signal line at -4.33, and the histogram is -0.31, so downside momentum is still building rather than fading. RSI 14 (a 0–100 momentum gauge; under 30 is oversold) is 24.48, which means oversold, but oversold readings can last a long time in a crash. Price sits right on the lower Bollinger Band at $11.70 (the bands mark roughly two standard deviations around the 20-day average of $20.65). The day\u0027s low of $11.75 is also the new 52-week low, about 83% below the 52-week high of $70.71. On 20 Nov the stock opened at its high of $14.07 and closed near its low on 131.3M shares, about 1.65x the 20-day average of 79.5M. That is heavy selling with no sign of buyers stepping in yet. Nearest resistance is the $14.07 open, then the Bollinger midline near $20.65.",
               "fundamental":  "IREN is moving from bitcoin mining to AI cloud, and the shift is showing up in revenue. In Q1 FY27 (quarter to 30 Sep, reported 5 Nov 2026), revenue was $228M, up 66% from the prior quarter. AI Cloud revenue was $200M, up 182%, with an 86% gross margin, and contracted backlog rose 22% to $20.3B. About two-thirds of that backlog is with investment-grade customers, including Microsoft under a roughly $9.7B, five-year contract at Childress. The weak point is the \u0027financing-dependent\u0027 half of the thesis. Net debt jumped to $4.43B from $216M in one quarter, $6B of the $15B remaining FY27 capex still has no funding, and management says capital markets have become \u0027more selective.\u0027 Owning powered land is still the core advantage, but how fast IREN can grow now depends on whether credit stays open.",
               "news":  [
                            "5 Nov 2026: Q1 FY27 revenue of $228M slightly beat consensus, and AI Cloud revenue nearly tripled to $200M. The loss per share (about $0.62–0.66, depending on the source) was wider than the roughly $0.48–0.49 expected, and the net loss was $263M.",
                            "5 Nov 2026: Management moved the \u003e$4B annualized AI Cloud revenue target from end-2026 to January 2027, possibly late February. Co-CEO Daniel Roberts blamed a network/design change requested by Microsoft and supplier timing, and said the contract is not at risk.",
                            "5 Nov 2026: Of $15B remaining FY27 capex, about $9B is covered and about $6B still has to be raised. IREN says the $6B is for capacity beyond the $4B target.",
                            "6 Nov 2026: Shares rose 20% to $21.64 on 112.9M shares. Since quarter-end IREN has signed a five-year contract in which the customer prepaid 90% of GPU capex, and contracted pricing rose 11–12%. Bloomberg separately reported that Microsoft is tightening oversight of some neocloud-run projects, including IREN\u0027s.",
                            "7–9 Nov 2026: Analysts cut their targets. Goldman Sachs went to $30 from $50 (Neutral), JPMorgan to $55 from $65 (Overweight) and Cantor Fitzgerald to $47 from $99. The stock fell about 10% to $19.34 on 10 Nov.",
                            "16 Nov 2026: IREN opened about 7% lower at $16.84 as leadership turmoil at NVIDIA and fresh doubts about how neoclouds fund themselves hit the group. Nebius and CoreWeave also fell.",
                            "20 Nov 2026: IREN fell 17.97% to $11.82 in a broad AI rout. The Nasdaq lost 5.13% and NVIDIA fell 14.01% after reports of an SEC accounting probe. There was no IREN-specific news that day. The annual general meeting is on 24 Nov 2026."
                        ],
               "caution":  "The main risk is funding. IREN has to raise about $6B while its shares are down about 83% from their peak and credit markets are tightening for neoclouds. New equity or convertible notes at these prices would dilute existing holders heavily. Debt costs are already rising: finance expense was $73M in Q1. Revenue relies heavily on Microsoft, which has already delayed one ramp and is reportedly tightening oversight. A beta of about 4.3 means IREN tends to fall several times harder than the market whenever the AI trade unwinds.",
               "opportunity":  "The business numbers and the share price are moving in opposite directions. AI Cloud revenue nearly tripled, margins are 86%, backlog grew to $20.3B, and a new customer prepaid 90% of GPU capex, a funding model that eases the financing burden. If the Microsoft capacity starts on time in January–February 2027 and IREN funds the $6B without heavy dilution, the market could start valuing it on its contracts again. RSI 24.48 and price at the lower band point to a stretched, oversold position.",
               "risks":  [
                             "About $6B of FY27 capex still unfunded while capital markets turn selective",
                             "Net debt up to $4.43B and rising interest costs",
                             "Customer concentration in Microsoft; further delays or tighter oversight",
                             "Heavy dilution if equity or convertibles are issued near 52-week lows",
                             "High beta (~4.3) to AI/semiconductor selloffs, such as the NVIDIA rout on 20 Nov",
                             "No confirmed bottom: falling MACD histogram and the low set on high volume"
                         ],
               "summary":  "IREN\u0027s operations improved in Q1 FY27: AI Cloud revenue tripled to $200M and backlog reached $20.3B. The stock, however, fell from $21.64 on 6 Nov to $11.82 on 20 Nov. Three things drove the drop: Microsoft-related delays to the $4B revenue goal, a $6B funding gap, and a sector-wide AI selloff. The technicals are bearish across the board, with price below all EMAs, negative MACD and a new 52-week low on heavy volume, though RSI is oversold. The moat of owned power sites is still there. The financing it depends on is now the thing to watch."
